  <h1>Google Ads for architects</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Somewhere right now, a marketing consultant is telling an architect that Google
    Ads is the answer because "that's where the intent is." He is not wrong. He is
    just skipping the part where intent costs ten dollars a click, the pool of
    searchers in your metro is finite, and every one of them has your three nearest
    competitors open in the next tabs.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is not a takedown. We buy ads for architecture firms for a living, and
    search ads have a real seat in the system. It is a smaller, sharper seat than
    the pitch suggests, and it only pays if you walk in knowing the numbers. So
    here are the numbers.
  </p>

  <div class="answer">
    <p class="answer-label">The short answer</p>
    <p>Google Ads works for architects as a capture channel. A residential click runs roughly $4 to $10, "commercial architect" about $19, and at the average search conversion rate an inquiry lands around $120. Buy it with tight keywords, a negative list, a dedicated landing page, and a budget that follows the season (March runs nearly twice the summer). Just don't call it a growth plan. Search only harvests people already looking, and on the eight architect searches we track, that pool was about a fifth smaller in January to October 2026 than a year earlier.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What you are actually buying</h2>
  <p>
    Google Ads, for a firm, mostly means one thing: paying to sit at the top of the
    results page when someone searches for what you do. You choose the searches, you
    bid per click, you pay when they click. The appeal is honest. These people are
    looking for an architect right now, today, with a project in hand. No other
    channel hands you that.
  </p>
  <p>
    But notice what the machine cannot do. It cannot make more people search. Search
    ads harvest demand that already exists, and the harvest is exactly as big as the
    field. You are renting position in a queue of fixed length, and so is everyone
    you bid against. Hold that thought, capture versus create, because it decides
    where this channel belongs in your growth. We will come back to it.
  </p>

  <h2>What Google Ads cost for architects</h2>
  <p>
    Pulled from Google's own advertising data for the US market on 16 November 2026. Volume is the
    12-month average (November 2025 to October 2026), the cost per click is Google's estimate, and the last column is the range
    between the slowest and busiest month in that window. Your metro sees a slice of each total:
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Search</th><th>US searches / month</th><th>Cost per click</th><th>Slowest to busiest month</th></tr>
    <tr><td>"architect near me"</td><td>~18,100</td><td>~$9.73, top-of-page bids $3.51 to $16.39</td><td>14,800 to 27,100</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"architecture firms near me"</td><td>~9,900</td><td>~$4.46</td><td>8,100 to 14,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"residential architect"</td><td>~6,600</td><td>~$10.17</td><td>3,600 to 9,900</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"residential architect near me"</td><td>~1,000</td><td>~$9.81</td><td>720 to 2,900</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"architect for home addition"</td><td>~1,000</td><td>~$24.89</td><td>260 to 1,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"commercial architect"</td><td>~2,900</td><td>~$18.63, top-of-page to $23.51</td><td>1,900 to 4,400</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"commercial architect near me"</td><td>~1,300</td><td>~$15.81, top-of-page to $21.53</td><td>720 to 2,400</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"how much does an architect cost"</td><td>~720</td><td>~$4.51</td><td>480 to 1,000</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    The prices moved in five weeks. Against our 8 October pull, Google's click estimate rose on seven of the eight searches: "commercial architect" went from $15.12 to $18.63, "architect for home addition" from $14.22 to $24.89, and "residential architect" from $8.29 to $10.17. Only "architect near me" eased, from $10.02 to $9.73.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now the napkin math. <a href="https://localiq.com/blog/search-advertising-benchmarks/" rel="nofollow">LocaliQ's 2026 search advertising
    benchmarks</a> (last updated 1 June 2026) put the average conversion rate from click to inquiry at 8.18
    percent across industries. Run "architect near me" through that: a hundred clicks costs about
    $1,000 and produces about eight inquiries, call it $120 per inquiry.
  </p>
  <p>
    And that is an inquiry, a form fill from a stranger, not a signed project. Not
    terrible. Also not the money printer the phrase "high intent" implies. For comparison, the
    median paid social lead across the 21 architecture firm accounts in our <a href="/architecture-marketing-benchmark-report-2026#the-benchmark-table">2026 benchmark report</a> cost $50, and the
    full side-by-side is <a href="/meta-ads-vs-google-ads-for-architects">Meta ads vs Google Ads for architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>When architects get searched: the 12-month curve</h2>
  <p>
    The averages in that table hide the most useful number in the whole data set: when the
    searches happen. We read the month-by-month counts behind each term (November 2025 to October 2026), and the swings are big enough to change how you budget.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>"Architect near me" peaked at 27,100 in March 2026 and sat at 14,800 every month from May through September, then rose to 18,100 in October. Spring runs nearly double the summer.</li>
    <li>"Commercial architect" ran from 1,900 (December 2025 and September 2026) to 4,400 (February, March and July 2026), better than a 2x swing.</li>
    <li>"Architect for home addition" ran 1,600 in March 2026 and fell to 260 in October.</li>
    <li>Not every curve agrees. "Residential architect" peaked at 9,900 in July and again in October 2026, and "how much does an architect cost" hit its high (1,000) in July, while "architect near me" sat at its low.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Two practical consequences. First, a flat monthly budget is wrong twice a year: in March
    the same daily cap runs out early while there are more searchers than you can afford, and
    in summer it chases a smaller pool. Second, a test run in July reads "architect near me" at
    its weakest. A firm that tries Google Ads for six summer weeks and concludes "it doesn't work
    here" may have measured the calendar, not the channel.
  </p>
  <p>
    One more measurement, taken live on 8 October 2026. We loaded the results page for "architect near
    me" and "commercial architect" in 12 US metros on desktop and mobile, 48 pages in all, and
    the Google map pack sat at the very top of 47 of them. Whatever you bid, your ad shares
    the screen with three free map listings, which is why the profile behind them, covered in <a href="/google-business-profile-for-architects#how-many-reviews-it-took-to-make-the-pack-in-12-us-cities">Google Business Profile for architects</a>, deserves its afternoon before the first ad dollar.
  </p>

  <h2>Fewer people searched for an architect in 2026</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the number that should change how you read every table on this page. We pulled 22 months of the same eight searches and added up January to October for each year. 2025 came to about 554,000 searches. 2026 came to about 435,000, a drop of 21.5 percent.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Search</th><th>Jan to Oct 2025</th><th>Jan to Oct 2026</th><th>Change</th></tr>
    <tr><td>"architect near me"</td><td>228,500</td><td>173,500</td><td>-24%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"architecture firms near me"</td><td>170,000</td><td>111,400</td><td>-34%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"residential architect"</td><td>60,500</td><td>75,900</td><td>+25%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"residential architect near me"</td><td>12,800</td><td>12,180</td><td>-5%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"architect for home addition"</td><td>12,380</td><td>8,660</td><td>-30%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"commercial architect"</td><td>40,600</td><td>32,700</td><td>-19%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"commercial architect near me"</td><td>20,900</td><td>14,100</td><td>-33%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"how much does an architect cost"</td><td>8,720</td><td>7,040</td><td>-19%</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    Google reports these counts in rounded steps (14,800, 18,100, 22,200), so read the direction, not the last digit. Seven of the eight point down. Put that next to the price moves above and the queue got shorter while the ticket got more expensive.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you do <strong>residential</strong> work, the generic searches shrank and the specific one grew. "Residential architect" is up a quarter, and "residential architect near me" hit 2,900 in October 2026, its busiest month in the 22 we pulled. "Architect for home addition" went the other way, from 1,900 in October 2025 to 260 in October 2026.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you do <strong>commercial</strong> work, the September spike did not come back. "Commercial architect" ran 6,600 in September 2025 and 1,900 in September 2026, tied for its slowest month of the year. In October we told firms on this page that commercial searches peak in September. That was one September of data, and the second one disagreed, so treat any single year's curve as a hint rather than a calendar.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our data cannot tell you why the searches fell. Some buyers may now ask an AI assistant the question they used to type into Google, which is a guess we have not measured. What we can measure is what those assistants say when a firm asks about Google Ads, which is the next section but one.
  </p>

  <h2>Who you are bidding against, counted at noon</h2>
  <p>
    That 8 October count ran at 3 AM Eastern, and it found only 2 ads in 48 pages, which says little at an hour when plenty of advertisers switch off. So we loaded the same 48 searches again at noon Eastern on Tuesday 13, Wednesday 14 and Thursday 15 October 2026: 144 first pages of Google, with every ad logged by position and by the company behind it.
  </p>
  <p>
    13 of the 144 pages carried any ad at all, about 9 percent. Those 13 pages held 18 ads, and only 2 sat at the top of the page. The other 16 ran at the bottom, below the map pack and the organic results. In seven of the 12 metros (New York, Chicago, Houston, Miami, Atlanta, Seattle and Denver) we did not see a single ad on either search on any of the three days.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Advertiser</th><th>What they are</th><th>Where we saw the ad</th><th>Ads</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Story Renovations</td><td>Custom home builder and renovator</td><td>"Architect near me", Phoenix and Dallas, all three days</td><td>5</td></tr>
    <tr><td>BuildEng</td><td>Engineering consultant (structural, MEP)</td><td>"Architect near me", Dallas, once at the top</td><td>3</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Ziese Architecture</td><td>Architecture firm, Los Angeles</td><td>"Commercial architect", Los Angeles desktop, once at the top</td><td>3</td></tr>
    <tr><td>VLK Architects</td><td>Architecture firm, Texas</td><td>"Commercial architect", Dallas and Austin desktop</td><td>2</td></tr>
    <tr><td>JFC Pros</td><td>Design-build general contractor, Massachusetts</td><td>"Commercial architect", Boston desktop</td><td>2</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Grand Street Engineering</td><td>Structural engineer, Kansas City</td><td>"Architect near me", Dallas</td><td>1</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Selkirk Design Company</td><td>Home designer, North Idaho</td><td>"Architect near me", Phoenix</td><td>1</td></tr>
    <tr><td>JLL</td><td>Commercial real estate services</td><td>"Commercial architect", Phoenix mobile</td><td>1</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    Two of the eight advertisers are architecture firms, and they placed 5 of the 18 ads. On most of these pages the paid slot was empty, and when it was filled, it was usually a builder, an engineer or a designer asking for the same client. Two of them were bidding a long way from home: a Kansas City engineer in Dallas and an Idaho home designer in Phoenix.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you do <strong>residential</strong> work, the rival in the paid slot is often a design-build company. Story Renovations ran an ad on "architect near me" on all three days, built around a free design consultation, and it was also one of the two ads in the 3 AM count. The homeowner who clicks it gets a builder offering free drawings, which is a harder pitch to beat than another architect's portfolio.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you do <strong>commercial</strong> work, "commercial architect" on desktop was the most contested combination we measured, and it still showed an ad on only 6 of 36 pages. Both architecture firms in the count bid here, Ziese Architecture in Los Angeles and VLK Architects in Dallas and Austin. On mobile the same search showed one ad in 36 pages, from JLL.
  </p>
  <p>
    Treat this as a snapshot. Each page is one load from one city at one minute, ads shift with the searcher and with the budget an advertiser has left that day, and Google's $10 click estimate is an average over a year of auctions, so an empty slot on a Tuesday does not make it a $2 click.
  </p>
  <p>
    What the count does show is that in most of these metros, on these three days, a firm bidding with tight keywords would have met very few people from its own profession. The pool of searchers is still the same size, which is the ceiling the rest of this page is about. A thin auction just makes the capture half of the engine easier to own.
  </p>

  <h2>What ChatGPT and Perplexity tell an architect Google Ads costs</h2>
  <p>
    On 16 November 2026 we asked ChatGPT (gpt-5.5) and Perplexity (sonar), both with web search on, four questions a firm owner actually asks: what Google Ads cost an architecture firm, whether a residential firm should run them, whether a commercial firm should, and what a small firm should budget. Eight answers, 46 cited pages.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Engine and question</th><th>Click price it quoted</th><th>Starting monthly budget</th></tr>
    <tr><td>ChatGPT, cost</td><td>$4 to $40+, by project type</td><td>$2,000 to $3,000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>ChatGPT, residential</td><td>"$8+" average</td><td>$1,500 to $3,000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>ChatGPT, commercial</td><td>"Mid-single digits to higher"</td><td>$1,500 to $10,000+, by market size</td></tr>
    <tr><td>ChatGPT, budget</td><td>No number</td><td>$1,500 to $3,000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Perplexity, cost</td><td>$5 to $15, commercial $10 to $25+</td><td>$1,000 to $3,000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Perplexity, residential</td><td>$4 to $10 (quoting this page)</td><td>None given</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Perplexity, commercial</td><td>$3 to $15, up to $40 in big metros</td><td>$1,500 to $10,000+</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Perplexity, budget</td><td>$15 to $40</td><td>$1,500 to $2,500</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    Set that against Google's own numbers in the cost table above. The highest average click estimate among our eight searches is $24.89, and the highest top-of-page bid Google reports is $23.51. Three of the eight answers still quoted clicks up to $40. Perplexity's "$15 to $40" traced to one marketing-school blog post dated 4 March 2026, which credits "industry benchmarks from WordStream and HubSpot Research" without naming a study.
  </p>
  <p>
    ChatGPT's "$12 to $40+" for luxury and design-build work had no source at all. The one page it cited was WordStream's 2026 benchmark, an average of $5.42 a click across industries, and the answer called that "lower than what I'd expect for architecture" before writing its own higher range. Two of the ten pages behind Perplexity's cost answer came from a UK agency and were priced in pounds.
  </p>
  <p>
    The budgets agree with each other far more than the click prices do. All seven answers that named one started a small firm at $1,000 to $2,000 a month in ad spend, before management fees. What none of the eight mentioned was the season or the drop. Not one told a firm that March runs nearly twice the summer, or that the pool it was budgeting for had shrunk by about a fifth in a year.
  </p>
  <p>
    One more thing, because it is ours. Perplexity's residential answer cited this page and repeated its numbers, $4 to $10 a click and roughly $120 an inquiry, along with its point that many homeowners never search for an architect until late. If you ask an AI about your own channel, read the sources it hands you. Some of them did the counting and some of them did not.
  </p>

  <h2>The two buyers search differently</h2>
  <p>
    If you do <strong>residential</strong> work, pay attention to when your buyer searches. A homeowner types "architect near me" late, usually after they own the land, have talked to a builder, and discovered they need drawings. The bigger residential truth is that many of your best future clients never search at all, because they do not realize an architect belongs this early in the process.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the search pool only ever contains the informed minority, and every firm in town that ranks or bids is competing for it. The sleeper keyword is the cost question: "how much does an architect cost" runs about 720 searches a month at roughly $4.32 a click, and the firm willing to answer it honestly on the landing page wins a nervous reader everyone else is dodging. The full picture of that buyer is in <a href="/marketing-for-residential-architects">marketing for residential architects</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you do <strong>commercial</strong> work, the click costs nearly twice as much for a reason. "Commercial architect" runs about $19, against $10 for "architect near me", because there are fewer searches and each one can be worth a building. The catch is that a lot of commercial work never touches a search box. Developers keep shortlists, and the firms already in the room get the call, which is the whole argument of <a href="/marketing-for-commercial-architects#how-to-get-in-front-of-them-before-the-shortlist-exists">marketing for commercial architects</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the searches that do happen are gold, worth owning, and worth patient follow-up, because a $19 click that goes quiet for four months is not dead, it is deciding. But you cannot scale a commercial pipeline on 2,900 national searches a month, and in September 2026 it was 1,900. The queue is short, however expensive the ticket.
  </p>

  <h2>The traps that eat small budgets</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The default settings. Google's setup flow steers small advertisers into automated campaigns and broad matching, which is a polite way of saying "we will decide what counts as a match for your money." Left alone, "architect" happily matches people researching software, salaries, and career changes. Tight keywords plus a negative list (free, software, salary, school, jobs) are the unglamorous twenty minutes that prevent most of the waste.</li>
    <li>Sending the click to your homepage. You paid ten dollars for a visitor with a project, and the homepage offers them a photo grid and a menu. The ad did its job, the landing fumbled it. That handoff is the whole subject of <a href="/website-design-for-architects#what-a-site-that-converts-actually-has">website design for architects</a>.</li>
    <li>Judging the channel on a hundred-dollar test. A hundred dollars buys about ten clicks. Ten clicks tell you nothing except that clicks are expensive.</li>
    <li>No follow-up behind the form. An inquiry that cost $120 and receives one reply before eternal silence is a donation to Google. The sequence that stops the leak is <a href="/email-marketing-for-architects#you-already-paid-for-these-leads-then-you-let-them-leak">email marketing for architects</a>.</li>
    <li>Having no ask at all. Both styles of offer work behind a search ad: a soft, low-friction ask (a fee guide, a plan review) that catches more people a little earlier, or a direct ask to book a consultation that catches fewer with more intent. Different systems, both valid. The only real mistake is a landing page that asks for nothing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Capture the searchers. Then go create the rest</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the distinction that should decide your budget. Google Ads captures demand: it reaches only the people who already knew to search, and the numbers above put a hard ceiling on how many of those exist.
  </p>
  <p>
    Paid social creates demand: it puts your work in front of the far larger group who are planning a project but have not typed anything yet, the homeowner still collecting ideas if you do residential, the developer whose next building has no architect attached if you do commercial.
  </p>
  <p>
    It reaches them before a shortlist exists, which is exactly why it scales where search plateaus, summer included. That full case is in <a href="/meta-ads-for-architects">Meta ads for architects</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    These are not rivals. They are two halves of the same paid engine: social fills the funnel, search catches the bottom of it, the website converts the attention, and the follow-up closes it. And while you rent the top of the results page, <a href="/seo-for-architects">SEO for architects</a> is how you slowly earn a permanent spot on it, so the rent goes down over time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Firms that grow run the whole loop on purpose, which is the system we map in <a href="/architecture-marketing">architecture marketing</a> and build out in <a href="/lead-generation-for-architects">lead generation for architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>A few honest answers</h2>
  <h3>Can we run Google Ads ourselves?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes, if you respect the boring parts: exact keywords, a negative list,
    conversion tracking, and a dedicated landing page instead of the homepage.
    The platform's defaults are designed around Google's revenue, not your
    outcome, so every recommendation it makes deserves a raised eyebrow. Skip the
    boring parts and you will run the classic experiment: spend a thousand
    dollars, collect clicks from people looking for free floor plans, conclude
    ads do not work.
  </p>
  <h3>How much do Google Ads cost for architects?</h3>
  <p>
    Arithmetic, not opinion: residential clicks run about $10 and commercial ones about
    $16 to $19, and you need a few hundred clicks before the results mean anything. That is
    roughly $3,000 over two or three months for residential terms, more for commercial. If that
    stings, it is still the going rate for real data. A smaller spend does not buy a smaller
    answer, it buys no answer.
  </p>
  <h3>When is the best time to run Google Ads for an architecture firm?</h3>
  <p>
    It depends on the search you buy, so check its own curve. "Architect near me" hit 27,100 searches in March 2026 against 14,800 in each month from May to September. "Commercial architect" was strongest in February, March and July 2026 and weakest in December and September (2025 looked different), and "residential architect" peaked in July and October. Run all year if the channel pays, weight the budget toward each term's busy months, and never judge a first test on a single season.
  </p>
  <h3>Are fewer people searching for architects on Google?</h3>
  <p>
    On the eight architect searches we track, yes. January to October 2026 came to about 435,000 US searches against about 554,000 for the same months of 2025, down 21.5 percent. "Architect near me" fell 24 percent and "commercial architect" 19 percent. The exception was "residential architect", up 25 percent. Google rounds these counts, so trust the direction more than the exact figure.
  </p>
  <h3>Are other architects running Google Ads in my city?</h3>
  <p>
    Fewer than you would guess. At noon Eastern on three weekdays in October 2026 we loaded "architect near me" and "commercial architect" in 12 US metros on desktop and mobile. Only 13 of 144 results pages showed any ad, seven metros showed none at all, and just two of the eight advertisers were architecture firms. Most of the bidders were builders, engineers and designers. Before assuming your auction is crowded, check your own terms in your own city with the Ad Preview and Diagnosis tool inside Google Ads, which shows the page without counting as a search.
  </p>
  <h3>Google Ads or Facebook ads: which is better for architects?</h3>
  <p>
    They do different jobs. Search reaches the informed minority already looking,
    and there are only so many of them. Social reaches the majority who have not
    started looking, which is why it keeps scaling after search flattens. A firm
    that wants growth rather than a trickle usually builds the demand engine
    first and adds capture on top, because capture alone plateaus at the size of
    the queue.
  </p>

  <h2>Rent the top of the page, on purpose</h2>
  <p>
    Google Ads is a good tool with a hard ceiling: real buyers, honest intent, priced by auction, capped by how many people search and when. Run it with tight keywords, a landing page that answers questions, a budget that follows the season, and follow-up behind it, and it earns its keep. Just do not ask it to be your growth plan.
  </p>
  <p>
    Growth comes from creating demand, not splitting a fixed pool with everyone else who bought the same keyword. Building that whole engine for architecture firms, creation and capture and everything after the click, is what we do all day.
  </p>
